Q1. What is Continuous Integration (CI)? Explain how it works.

Interview Ready Answer

Continuous Integration (CI) is a DevOps practice in which developers
frequently integrate their code changes into a shared repository. Every
code commit automatically triggers a build and automated tests. If any
build or test fails, developers receive immediate feedback so that
issues can be fixed early before they affect other developers.

Definition

Continuous Integration (CI) is the practice of frequently merging code
changes into a shared repository where every change is automatically
built and tested.

Purpose

• Detect integration issues early.
• Improve software quality.
• Reduce manual testing.
• Reduce merge conflicts.
• Provide immediate feedback.
• Speed up software development.

======================================================================

Q2. Explain the workflow of Continuous Integration (CI).

Interview Ready Answer

Whenever a developer commits code to the shared repository, the CI server
automatically detects the change. It builds the application and executes
automated tests. If the build or tests fail, the developer is notified
immediately. If they pass, the code is considered ready for the next
stage of the software delivery pipeline.

CI Workflow

Developer Writes Code
        │
        ▼
Commit & Push Code
        │
        ▼
Shared Repository (Git)
        │
        ▼
CI Server Detects Change
(Jenkins / GitHub Actions / GitLab CI)
        │
        ▼
Automatic Build
        │
        ▼
Automatic Testing
        │
        ▼
Pass / Fail Report
        │
        ▼
Developer Fixes Issues (if any)

Important Point

CI stops after build verification and automated testing.

Deployment is NOT part of Continuous Integration.

======================================================================

Q3. What problem does Continuous Integration solve?

Interview Ready Answer

Before Continuous Integration, developers worked independently for long
periods and integrated their code only at the end of development. This
caused large merge conflicts, delayed bug detection, manual testing, and
slow software releases.

Continuous Integration solves these problems by requiring developers to
integrate code frequently. Every code change is automatically built and
tested, allowing issues to be detected and fixed immediately before they
become larger problems.

Problems Before CI

• Large merge conflicts.
• Delayed bug detection.
• Manual testing.
• Slow software releases.
• Difficult integration of multiple developers' code.
• Increased development time.

How CI Solves Them

• Frequent code integration.
• Automatic build.
• Automatic testing.
• Immediate feedback.
• Early bug detection.
• Reduced merge conflicts.
• Improved software quality.

